# Overview
#
# Each phrase announces that the writer is about to say a thing again,
# so the reader reads one point twice. This tier reports `In short,` as
# a sentence opener that announces a summary (`In short, the cache is a
# map`). The fix makes the first statement stand alone and deletes the
# phrase. The rule sits at Recommended, because the opener also leads a
# point that the text has not made yet.
#
# Expression
#
# `in short` counts only with the glued comma of an opener. Weir cannot
# anchor a branch to the start of a sentence, so the phrase also counts
# as an aside between two commas (`The answer, in short, is no`). The
# aside announces the same summary, and the same fix applies.
#
# False Positives
#
# Without the comma, `short` describes the noun after it (`memory is in
# short supply`), so the phrase passes.
#
# `in short,` before a second adjective still fires (`write in short,
# plain sentences`), because no guard reads the word after the comma.
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Required ProseRestatement holds every other restatement.
#
# ProseSentenceAdverb reports `Ultimately,`, so this rule leaves the
# opener out. `Ultimately,` announces a summary less often than it
# leads a point that the paragraph has not made yet (`Ultimately, your
# applications run as containers inside Pods`).
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 4 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 3 of 4 announced a
# second statement of a point that the text had made. One hit summed up
# its paragraph. The other two followed a sentence that named a term
# (`Rust performs two's complement wrapping`), and they stated the same
# behavior in plain words. In the misfire, the opener led the answer to
# a question that the paragraph before it asked (`why is having
# Option<T> any better than having null? In short, because`).
#
# `Ultimately,` hit 9 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 2 of 9
# announced a summary. In 6 hits, the opener led a point that the
# paragraph had not made yet. In the other hit, `ultimately,` sat
# between two commas in the middle of a sentence (`and so, ultimately,
# hold different types`).
expr main (in short,)
let message "Restatement. Make the first statement stand alone, and delete the phrase."
let description "Flags `in short` as a sentence opener that announces a summary."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Memory is in short supply on the build host."
allows "Ultimately, the parser decides which branch runs."
